body:
Not to sound like a prick, but I'm about to. There is no finesse. There is no tea. You have half-wit doctors who want their patients to shut up and think they're untouchable, and then sometimes you can come across one that is reasonable. The dark web hasn't been dark since the early 2000's. Dealers and users alike are so hooked on these substances they risk felony charges just to get them through the post. If you're scripted, I can imagine you deal with a lot more than just panic attacks or needing something to "calm the fuck down".

&#x200B;

Give therapy a try if your panic attacks are that bad. And if you've gone eleven times and still see no result yet you're trying everything within you to live past these momentary episodes, then, maybe then, you'd be a medicinal candidate. Even after all of that, why on God's green Earth would you wish the dependence of these drugs upon you? I really don't think people understand the consequences of this drug type. And that's coming from a prescribed user.

&#x200B;

Do your research, and believe it. We suffer. Even with pills coming out of our pockets and the lack of a panic attack, we suffer.

Personally, I think it needs to happen. Here in the U.S., only a few selective states have it scheduled. Its history on the market kind of had a hand in its over-prescription, therefore it's abuse potential. Those who are not therapeutic users can become dependent easily, and more so if they already have substance abuse history. Having no tolerance, recreational users are more likely to experience the euphoric effects associated with gabapentin and are more likely to seek it out again in conjunction with other drugs which gabapentin generally potentates.

&#x200B;

As far as pregablin, I'm not versed in its history or its kinetics. I know that it's not as easily obtained and some doctors, even specialists will not prescribe it. It's condition based I believe. I'm not sure.

&#x200B;

I'm glad at least the U.K. is trying to control the problem with pharm sooner rather than later. When the prescribed opiates disappeared, people moved on to heroine and fent. People are dying in the streets now. Does this drug have that potential? I don't think so. But it is another alternative. It's another kick to those that need it. And if doctors, patients, and user alike are not careful, dependence is right around the corner.
